---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="How scoring works" description="A plain-language guide to how GameBench builds, tests, verifies, and publishes model-made games.">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="eyebrow">Scoring without magic</span>
    <h1>The published score comes from the machine contract.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Deterministic browser checks measure whether the delivered game follows
      its public rules. Optional human-review summaries appear only when data is
      attached, and never change the machine score.
    </p>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="grid cols-3">
        <article class="card">
          <span class="pill">Build score</span>
          <h3>Did the model make working games?</h3>
          <p>Six new-game challenges test installation, controls, mechanics, game state, reset behavior, and stability.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="pill">Reproduce score</span>
          <h3>Could it remake an existing game?</h3>
          <p>Two reference-game challenges test behavioral fidelity and visual structure under the original license.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="pill">Compatibility metric</span>
          <h3>Core is no longer the primary board</h3>
          <p>From v0.5, Build is the headline ranking and Reproduce is reported separately. Core remains visible only for schema and historical compatibility.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">From prompt to score</span><h2>Five steps, all inspectable.</h2></div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid cols-3 how-grid">
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>One development invocation</h3><p>Each model gets the same starter project, public task contract, time budget, and allowed network policy, then submits source once.</p></article>
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>Frozen submission</h3><p>The delivered source is archived before evaluation. No new agent attempt is made for a different evaluation seed.</p></article>
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>Three seed evaluations</h3><p>The browser evaluates that same submission under three fixed seeds using real keyboard, pointer, bridge, and state checks.</p></article>
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>Every game counts equally</h3><p>No favorite game gets a secret bonus. Scores are averaged per game, then per track.</p></article>
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>Clean rebuild</h3><p>Before publication, private logs and credentials are removed. The clean source must rebuild and produce the same score.</p></article>
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>Versioned interpretation</h3><p>v0.5 ranks Build first and reports Reproduce separately. Historical v0.3 pages retain their original Core and three-attempt meaning.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Official vs experimental</span><h2>“Published” does not always mean “ranked.”</h2></div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid cols-3">
        <article class="card"><span class="pill experimental">Experimental</span><h3>Useful measured evidence</h3><p>May have partial task or seed coverage, or lack independent operator verification. It is public but never mixed into the Official board.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><span class="pill official">Official</span><h3>One build, complete evaluation</h3><p>Each task is developed once and the frozen submission is evaluated under all three fixed seeds. Rebuild, evidence, environment, and network isolation are verified.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><span class="pill">Separate tracks</span><h3>Build leads; Reproduce stands alone</h3><p>The v0.5 leaderboard sorts by Build. Reproduce remains visible as its own capability instead of being hidden inside a headline Core score.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose">
      <h2>Technical audit notes</h2>
      <p>The short version above is enough to understand a score. The details below exist for researchers and result auditors.</p>

      <details class="audit">
        <summary>Version and identity rules</summary>
        <div class="audit-body">
          <p>A Benchmark release freezes every task version and content hash, browser bridge protocol, scoring contract, seed policy, and aggregation rule. Scores from different releases are never ranked together.</p>
          <p>From v0.5, each development invocation has a <code>submission_id</code>; each seed evaluation has its own <code>run_id</code>. Historical pages preserve the earlier attempt-based identity model.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="audit">
        <summary>Deterministic browser contract</summary>
        <div class="audit-body">
          <p>Tests combine native keyboard and pointer events with a public bridge that controls reset, time, actions, and state snapshots. The snapshot must report the active seed and match the task schema.</p>
          <p>Dependency install, build, server start, bridge preflight, and the initial snapshot are hard gates. Any failure makes the complete task zero.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="audit">
        <summary>Official publication checklist</summary>
        <div class="audit-body">
          <p>Official seeds are <code>104729</code>, <code>130363</code>, and <code>155921</code>. The operator exports secret-free source, rebuilds in a digest-pinned evaluator image, recomputes the score, verifies the evidence manifest, and records network isolation.</p>
          <p>The development deadline freezes the submitted source. Its three seed evaluations run afterward without invoking the coding agent again; evaluation errors cannot fill an Official result cell.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
